{"about":"6b78e82f822685c4d309409a6bc6f5d2b5f5ce30f34f7c6e189eb91c1949ad28","body":"The conjecture claims the preference is a 'rational bet' to maximize learning potential, but this smuggles in an inductive assumption about the value of future learning. To assert that surviving severe tests makes a theory a better vehicle for *future* information gain presupposes that the future will resemble the past regarding the theory's testability and the relevance of those tests. If the universe is chaotic or if the laws change tomorrow, the 'high content' theory might yield no more learning than a weak one, or could even be misleadingly complex. By betting on high content because it has worked well so far to define 'severe tests,' Popper relies on the very uniformity of nature he explicitly rejects to justify the pragmatic utility of his methodological rule.","kind":"mini.criticism.v1"}